== A Master’s Correcting and Governing of His Household

=== §. 13. #emph[Of the power of masters to correct their servants.]

The second point in which a master’s power consists is #emph[correction,] which may be given by looks, words or deeds.

By a man’s look his anger and wrath against another is shown. #footnote[אף ab אנף] In Hebrew the same word means a face and wrath, because wrath shows itself soonest in a man’s face. It is noted of Cain that he “became very angry, and his countenance fell”#footnote[#emph[Si quis in domo per inobedientiam domesticae paci aduersatur, corripiatur seu verbo, seu verbere, seu quolibet alio genere poenae iusto atque licito. Aug. de Ciu. Dei l.] 19. #emph[c.] 16. \[If anyone in the household opposes the peace of the household by disobedience, let him be corrected by word, or by blow, or by any other kind of punishment that is just and lawful.\]] (Gen 4:5). Now the showing of a master’s wrath against his servant is a correction.

But words, whether of #emph[rebuke] or of #emph[threatening,] show it much more. This phrase which Solomon uses in Prov 29:19 (“a servant will not be corrected by words”) shows that there is a correction by words. And though it is put negatively, it does not imply that correction by words is not to be used with a servant, but rather that, if he is not moved by it, blows must be added to it. This is a correction by deeds, which Christ mentions in the parable of the servants who, according to the greatness of their fault, are #footnote[Luke 12:47.] to be “beaten with many blows.” It is therefore in a master’s power to correct his servant with stripes or blows. This being so, I will show:

+ How far his power extends in this.
+ How it is to be ordered.

=== §. 14. #emph[Of the restraint of masters’ power: that it does not reach to their servants’ lives.]

Concerning the extent of a master’s power in correcting his servant, this question must be resolved: #emph[Whether a master has power to take away his servant’s life for any fault.]

#emph[Answer.] His power does not reach so far, as is evident from these reasons:

1\. There is no precept, no approved example, nor any other warrant for it in God’s word. The Jews had great power over servants who were foreigners. From them they might #footnote[Lev 25:44, 45, 46.] buy male and female slaves; they might have them as a possession, and take them as an inheritance for their children after them, to be slaves forever. They might be put to the most toilsome, drudging, base and lowly work they had, such as drawing water, cutting wood and the like; but even so their masters #footnote[Josh 9:27.] had no power over their lives.

2\. A master was not allowed to maim his servant: if he struck out an eye or a tooth of his servant unintentionally, he had to make amends, which was to let him go free.#footnote[Exod 21:26.] Much less, therefore, was he allowed to take away his servant’s life.

3\. If a servant died under his master’s hand when the master was correcting him, though he did not intend willfully to murder him, that master was to be punished.#footnote[Exod 21:20.] It was not lawful, therefore, for a master knowingly to kill a servant.

4\. The power of life belongs only to the public magistrate, who does all things in open, public places, so that there may be many witnesses of his just proceeding. If masters had this power, many might be put to death secretly, and no man would know for what cause, as happens in popish inquisitions.

5\. The#footnote[#emph[Si Dominus seruum ceciderit, vel lethale vulnus inflixerit, reus homicidij sit. Justin. Cod. l.] 9. #emph[tit.] 14.] approved laws of men make it willful murder for a master to kill his servant knowingly, however heinous the servant’s fault. Neither the authority of the master nor what the servant deserves will exempt the master who kills his servant from the guilt and punishment of felony.

#emph[Objection.] In ancient times masters had this power.

#emph[Answer.] They never had it by right, though some may have exercised it. Among God’s people it was never exercised in any age of the world. The liberty that was taken was among the heathen; and yet among them, as states came to be governed with more #footnote[#emph[Apud Gentes animaduertere possumus Dominis in seruos vitae, necisque potestatem fuisse. Sed hoc tempore nullis hominibus qui sub imperio nostro sunt, licet sine causa legibus cognita in seruos suos supra modum saeuire Nam ex constit. Antonini qui sine causa seruum suum occiderit, non minus puniri iubetur, quam si alienum seruum occiderit. Iustin. Jnstit. l.] 1. #emph[Tit.] 8. \[Among the nations we may observe that masters had power of life and death over their servants. But at this time no men under our rule are permitted to rage beyond measure against their servants without a cause known to the laws. For by a constitution of Antoninus, whoever kills his own servant without cause is ordered to be punished no less than if he had killed another man’s servant.\] #emph[Domini mitiores esse debent. Cypr. Testim. l.] 3. #emph[c.] 73. \[Masters ought to be gentler.\]] and more civilized order, that usurped liberty was much restrained by the laws of magistrates; and when emperors and kings became Christians, it was taken away altogether.

#emph[Objection.] If a man takes an enemy in war, he has power to kill him.

#emph[Answer.] If he does not kill him in the time of the war, but spares him then, and takes him as a captive and makes him his servant, even though only a slave, he does not have power over his life.

=== §. 15. #emph[Of masters’ excess in correcting servants.]

Those act contrary to their just and due power who in their rage stab their servants or otherwise do away with them; and so do those who beat them so mercilessly and immoderately with rod, cudgel or anything else that death follows. For there are many who, having once begun to strike, do not know when to stop, but lay on as if they were striking stocks and blocks, and not their own flesh. God foresaw that masters were prone to such cruelty, and therefore #footnote[Deut 25:3.] set a fixed number of stripes, which no one who beat another was allowed to exceed.

Among these may be counted such reckless masters as in a fit of temper will strike their servant with anything that comes to hand, whether it is heavy, jagged, hard or sharp; they do not care#footnote[Prov 26:18.] (“like a madman shooting firebrands and deadly arrows”). These things may endanger a servant’s life; if not, they may break his head, or otherwise wound, bruise and lame him.

It is beyond a master’s power by any correction to impair the life, health or strength of his servant, or in any way to disable him in his body from doing what he otherwise might have been able to do.

If masters must not take away or endanger the life of their servant, no, not even as punishment for any sin, what may we think of masters who, without any fault in their servants, cause them to be killed by putting them on some desperate venture, either to pursue their own quarrel or for some other unjust end? David#footnote[2 Sam 11:15.] dealt in this way with Uriah; but afterward he #footnote[Ps 51:14.] bitterly repented of this piece of injustice. At another #footnote[2 Sam 23:15, #emph[etc.]] time, when three of his servants had fetched him water that he longed for at the risk of their lives, though they returned safely, yet his heart struck him for his longing, and he would not drink that water, because they had risked their lives to fetch it.

But what may we say of masters who cause their servants, for the masters’ sakes, to commit felony, murder, treason, rebellion and other such things as make the public magistrate unsheathe his sword against them and cut them off? We noted this #footnote[§. 8.] before as a grievous fault because of the unlawfulness of the thing; here we may further note that it is much more heinous because of the harm that follows from it, which is the loss of their servants’ lives. So in this way they make themselves accessories to a detestable sin, and guilty of the blood of their servant.

=== §. 16. #emph[Of masters ordering the correction they give to their servants.]

2\. So that masters may rightly order the correction which they give to their servants, a difference must be made between the age, sex, disposition and faults of those whom they correct.

1\. Masters ought not to be as ready to strike those who are grown in years as the younger sort. Years bring understanding, and a rebuke will make a man of understanding sorrier for a fault, and more concerned to amend it, than blows;#footnote[The direction prescribed to parents (Treatise 6, §. 47.) for rightly ordering the correction which they give to their children may be fitly applied here in many points. Read it.] pain works more on the younger sort. But if, despite their years, they are defiant and will not regard words, their defiance must be beaten down with blows. “Strike a mocker,”#footnote[Prov 19:25, 29.] says the wise man; and again, “Judgments are prepared for mockers, and beatings for the backs of fools.”

Since servants in years must in this case be corrected, it is further needful to make a difference between the kind or measure of correction given to them and to the younger sort. If they are corrected like children, they may either make light of it, or scorn it all the more. “Bruises, wounds and stripes that pierce the inmost parts are a purging medicine against evil”#footnote[Prov 20:30. #emph[Heris in famulos saeuitia adhibenda est, si aliter teneri non possunt. Cic. Offic. l.] 2. \[Masters must use severity toward their servants if they cannot be kept in check otherwise.\]] for defiant servants of ripe years.

2\. If there is a master and a mistress governing a house jointly, it is fittest for the master to correct the menservants, and the mistress the maids. #footnote[#emph[Seruam verberare viro maximae ignominiae est. Chrys. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 Cor 11. \[For a man to beat a maidservant is the greatest disgrace.\]] Abraham handed his maid over to Sarah in such a case. Yet if a maid should grow defiant and mannish, and turn against her mistress, the mistress being weak, sickly, with child, or otherwise unable to master her maid, the master may and must beat down the maid’s defiance and rebellion: #footnote[Exod 21:20.] so much the law of God permitted.

3\. If servants are of a frank and generous disposition, willing and eager to do what belongs to them, sorry when they have committed a fault, and concerned to amend their faults, many things may be passed over in them which must be corrected in others. To this may be applied the counsel of the wise man: “Do not pay attention to every word #footnote[Eccl 7:21.] that is spoken.”

4\. Correction must be measured according to the greatness #footnote[#emph[—Adsit Regula peccatis quae poenas irroget aequas: Nec scutica dignum horribili sectere flagello. Horat. l.] 1. #emph[Sat.] 3. \[Let there be a rule to assign penalties equal to the offenses, and do not flay with the horrible scourge one who deserves the strap.\]] of the fault punished, and the circumstances by which the fault may justly be made heavier. “That servant who knows his master’s will but does not … follow his instructions will be beaten with many blows. But the one who unknowingly does things worthy of punishment will be beaten with few blows” (Luke 12:47, 48).

Many failings are committed daily contrary to every branch of this direction, in that masters and mistresses, in exercising this part of their power, are carried away by anger, and do what they do in this way “at their own pleasure.” So they turn a duty into sin, and by undue correction of their servants provoke God to correct them in his wrath, either here or in the world to come.

=== §. 17. #emph[Of masters’ power over their servants in and about their marriage.]

The third point to be noted concerning a master’s power over his servant is about his servant’s marriage. Various questions are raised on this point, which I will briefly resolve.

1\. #emph[Question.] Do masters have power to order and arrange their servants’ marriages as they please?

#emph[Answer.] No, not without the free consent of the servants themselves; for marriages must be made with the free consent of the parties who marry.

#emph[Objection.] The law implies that a master has power to give #footnote[Exod 21:4.] his servant a wife, for it shows whose the children shall be who are born to the servant to whom a master has given a wife.

#emph[Answer.] 1. That law is to be understood of servants who, being foreigners, were slaves, over whom masters had a more absolute power than over others.

2\. The master’s power of giving did not of itself force the servant to marry the person so given, but restrained the servant from marrying anyone other than the one whom the master should give.

2\. #emph[Question.] Is a master’s power in the matter of marriage as great over a servant as a parent’s over a child?

#emph[Answer.] No. See the reason in Treatise 5, §. 20.

3\. #emph[Question.] May a master deny his servant liberty to marry?

#emph[Answer.] Yes, for the time for which the servant has contracted to serve his master. For that time a servant is part of his master’s goods and possessions. As slaves were a master’s possession forever, so contracted servants are his possession for the term of their contract. When God gave the devil leave to seize all that Job had,#footnote[Job 1:15, 16.] by virtue of that permission he seized all kinds of Job’s servants, slave and free, as well as his goods; which he could not have done if Job’s servants had not been counted as his goods.

Yet nevertheless, if servants make it known to their master that necessity requires them to marry, such regard ought to be had for the chastity even of servants that in this case I may use the phrase which the apostle uses in reference to children: “they should get married.” #footnote[1 Cor 7:36.]

4\. #emph[Question.] If servants marry without their masters’ consent, is the marriage made void by it?

#emph[Answer.] No. The marriage, being otherwise rightly performed, #footnote[Quod fieri non debet factum valet.] remains a firm marriage, though the servants have sinned in doing so, for which their master may justly punish them.

5\. #emph[Question.] May a master keep his servants who have married in this way without his consent from their spouses?

#emph[Answer.] He may demand the whole time of their contract for service; but the power which husbands and wives have over one another’s bodies by the bond of marriage does not allow #footnote[1 Cor 7:3, 4.] them to be wholly separated from one another. Besides, our law frees a maidservant from her master’s contract when she is married.

=== §. 18. #emph[Of masters’ severity in forcing marriages on servants, or in separating man and wife.]

Those act contrary to a master’s power who force their servants to marry someone they do not like: as when a master has defiled his maid, and to cover his shame he will make one of his servants marry her.

Those also act contrary to their power who seek to separate their married servants from their spouses. Some will keep the man at their house day and night away from his wife, and likewise the wife from her husband. Others will send one of them into remote parts of the land, where the other will not know. Indeed, others will make sure to keep them apart, for they will send one of them overseas for many years on end, if not forever. These practices are against the law of marriage, and too harsh and unjust.

=== §. 19. #emph[Of masters’ power to have the disposal of their servants’ persons.]

The fourth point to be noted about a master’s power over his servants concerns their persons, which belong so fully to a master as his own for the time of their service that he may not only keep them himself for his own service, but also pass them on, and give or sell them to another. By God’s law not only #footnote[Lev 25:39, 44.] foreigners but also Jews might be sold as servants.

The customs and statutes of our land also permit masters #footnote[Masters’ care in passing on servants.] to transfer their servants from one to another, and on their deathbeds to bequeath them to whomever they will, just like their goods and possessions.

So that this power is not abused, masters in exercising it must chiefly regard the good of their servants, and to that end pass them on to fit masters, such masters as may do them good and not evil all the time they stay with them and serve under them.

Contrary to this are those who aim merely at their own advantage, not caring to whom they pass on their servants, so long as they can make a profit by it. Some will sell them, namely when they have them overseas, to Turks and unbelievers; some to papists and other idolaters; some to profane persons; some to cruel, inhuman beasts; some to men of unlawful trades; some to men of no trade. Masters who make their servants over to such as these, or to men like them, will answer for all the wrong that is done to them.

=== §. 20. #emph[Of masters managing their authority well.]

The same apostle in #footnote[Col 4:1 τὸ δίκαιον καὶ τὴν ἰσότητα.] another place gives this charge to masters: “Give your#super[+] servants what is just and equal.”

By doing these two things masters will manage their authority well.

#emph[Justice] has respect to the place and service of servants. #footnote[Difference between just and equal dealing.]

#emph[Equity] has respect to their mind and manner of doing service.

All servants, in that they are servants and do their masters’ work, must have what by right belongs to servants. This is #emph[Justice.]

Servants who bear a special love and liking to their masters, doing service not by constraint or with eye-service, as men-pleasers, but in singleness of heart with good will and all good faithfulness, seeking their masters’ good to the utmost of their power, must be respected and dealt with accordingly. This is #emph[Equity.] As the apostle includes #emph[Justice] under this phrase #emph[(do the same things)] (for service, give due recompense), so more especially he includes #emph[Equity] under it: for servants’ good will and more than usual respect toward their masters, let masters return good will and more than ordinary respect to servants. Saint Peter notes these two virtues under two other words, #emph[good] #footnote[1 Pet 2:18.] and #emph[gentle:] a master’s #emph[goodness] relates to #emph[Justice,] his #emph[gentleness] to #emph[Equity.]
